  "notes": "From the article's garden views (photo 01: bedroom prisms stepping forward on the west with deep-set glass doors, the living room glazed full width behind a covered terrace, a tall pier at its east end, the big tree at the junction, planting beds along the walls; palms at the garden's edge; a field-stone boundary wall with bougainvillea in the living-room view), the street views (photo 09: closed tinted-concrete prisms of clearly different heights - 3.3 to 6.4 m - with lower walls between them, pale gravel with agaves and big-leaf plants along their feet, a dark stone gabion pillar, a narrow access alley with a dark steel frame) and the patio views (slate slabs in gravel, lush planting, wooden doors in the walls). This pass: the massing was one merged lump of four heights; it is now separate prisms with gaps - a street row of five blocks from 3.2 to 6.4 m, the alley between the first two with its steel portal and slab path, an entry patio behind the street row with slabs, planting and two wooden doors, a raised dining box seen over the living roof, a west wing of three bedroom prisms (3.8-4.4 m) with planted gaps, an east wing of two, and a small outbuilding. The glass now reads: every opening is a recess with the glass, half a metre of room and a dark interior behind it (glass is drawn open, so something must be behind it to show), and the living room's glass wall has dark mullions at about 2.6 m under a 1.8 m-deep terrace roof. Palms and an umbrella tree replace plain round trees where the photos show them; the boundary wall is rubble with bougainvillea (drawn in rose). Guessed: orientation (street north, garden south toward the Tepozteco), the whole plan and every block's size (estimated from door and storey heights - no plan is published), the terrace depth (kept shallow enough for the glass to show from above), how the alley reaches the patio, the boundary wall's line, the number of bedrooms per wing. Refined 24 Sep 2026 (three rounds against photos 00-10): the living room's glass wall is now taller (3.2 m) under a thinner roof edge, with a shallower 1.6 m overhang so the glazing reads from above instead of vanishing into shade; the bedroom prisms' glass doors are wider (about 3.8 m, as photos 00 and 05 show them nearly filling the end walls); the junction tree is bigger and taller (11.5 m, 6 m crown) as the wide, airy tree that dominates photos 01 and 07 (a leafless 'bare' crown was tried and read as a dead tree); more planting beds at the prisms' feet; the street gabion drawn dark slate."
